-- title:
--   Non-trivial inertia at q ∥ M on a Hecke eigenplane
-- statement:
--   Let $M \ge 1$ and let $g$ be a cusp form of weight $2$ on $\Gamma_0(M)$ which is a newform, i.e. $g$ is a normalised eigenform ($a_1(g)=1$, $a_{mn}=a_ma_n$ for coprime $m,n$, and the usual two-term recursions at prime powers, separately for $p \nmid M$ and $p \mid M$) and no proper divisor of $M$ supports a normalised eigenform with the same $q$-coefficients at all primes not dividing $M$. Let $\lambda$ be prime, $S$ a finite set of naturals, and $\chi_g$ a ring homomorphism from the Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra M 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) (the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb C} S_2(\Gamma_0(M))$ generated by the $T_\ell$ with $\ell \nmid M$, $\ell \notin S$ and the $U_q$ with $q \mid M$, $q \notin S$) to $\mathbb C$ sending each such $T_\ell$ to $a_\ell(g)$. Let $\mathcal O''$ be a commutative $\mathbb Z_\lambda$-algebra, $K$ a field of characteristic zero and $\mathcal O''$-algebra, and $\varphi$ a ring homomorphism from the image of $\chi_g$ to $K$. Give $J_0(M)$, the degree-zero Picard group of the modular function field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, its Hecke action by [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleBar`](def/ModularCurve_HeckeModule.html#L82), and let $T =$ [`ModularCurve.TateModule lam (JZero M)`](def/ModularCurve_EichlerShimuraData.html#L15) be the module of sequences $x$ with $x_0=0$ and $\lambda x_{n+1}=x_n$. Assume given: a $\mathbb Z_\lambda$-module structure on $T$ whose scalar action is levelwise multiplication by the representative of $a$ modulo $\lambda^n$; a multiplicative map $\rho_M$ from $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ to $\operatorname{End}_{\mathcal O''}(\mathcal O'' \otimes_{\mathbb Z_\lambda} T)$ with $\rho_M(\sigma)(a \otimes x) = a \otimes y$ whenever $y$ is the levelwise $\sigma$-translate of $x$; a ring homomorphism $T_M$ from [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlg`](def/HeckeGalois_EichlerShimura.html#L14) $= \mathbb Z[X_\ell]$ to the same endomorphism ring with $T_M(t)(a \otimes x) = a \otimes t\cdot x$; and a $K$-submodule $W$ of $K \otimes_{\mathcal O''}(\mathcal O'' \otimes_{\mathbb Z_\lambda} T)$ with $\dim_K W = 2$, stable under every base-changed $\rho_M(\sigma)$, on which the base change of $T_M(X_\ell)$ acts as the scalar $\varphi(\chi_g(T_\ell))$ for every prime $\ell \nmid M$ with $\ell \notin S$. Then for every prime $q \ne \lambda$ with $q \mid M$ and $q^2 \nmid M$, and every valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $q$ a non-unit of $A$, there exist $\tau$ in the inertia subgroup of $A$ over $\mathbb Q$ (the image in $\operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ of the inertia subgroup inside the decomposition subgroup) and $w \in W$ with $(\rho_M \tau)$ base-changed to $K$ sending $w$ to something different from $w$.
--
--   This is the non-vanishing monodromy half of local–global compatibility at a prime exactly dividing the level: the $\lambda$-adic Hecke eigenplane attached to a weight-two newform on $\Gamma_0(M)$ is ramified at each prime $q \parallel M$ with $q \ne \lambda$. It feeds the construction of the associated $\lambda$-adic Galois representation with non-trivial inertia and prescribed Frobenius action on the inertia-fixed line at such $q$, which in turn supplies the local behaviour needed at primes exactly dividing the level in the modularity argument.
